00:05:08.270 --> 00:05:09.228
- [Interview] And what compels you

00:05:09.228 --> 00:05:10.765
to get involved in a project?

00:05:10.765 --> 00:05:12.040
I know that's kind of a hard question,

00:05:12.040 --> 00:05:14.085
because it's probably so
many different things,

00:05:14.085 --> 00:05:17.643
but when you think about projects that you

00:05:17.643 --> 00:05:20.922
get involved with, what are
some of the key elements?

00:05:20.922 --> 00:05:24.206
- Well, I think, it can be really anything

00:05:24.206 --> 00:05:28.490
that gets you interested,
I just want to make sure

00:05:28.490 --> 00:05:30.582
I'm going to be challenged the whole time,

00:05:30.582 --> 00:05:34.082
because I know if I'm not given obstacles,

00:05:35.718 --> 00:05:38.599
I get kind of lazy, I don't really engage

00:05:38.599 --> 00:05:41.580
in the way I want to, so, if I know

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:45.330
something's going to constantly
be throwing challenges at me,

00:05:45.330 --> 00:05:47.749
not in like, I want to have fun,

00:05:47.749 --> 00:05:49.450
I always want to enjoy what I'm doing,

00:05:49.450 --> 00:05:52.033
but something that is going to be,

00:05:53.309 --> 00:05:55.265
that's part of the fun,
I think, is getting

00:05:55.265 --> 00:05:57.598
all of the challenges of it.

00:05:58.691 --> 00:06:02.492
- I took this big risk and quit school.

00:06:02.492 --> 00:06:05.742
I quit the one thing that was surefire,

00:06:06.959 --> 00:06:11.748
and went and became a
model, and got really bored

00:06:11.748 --> 00:06:15.078
really fast, and didn't
love being a coat hanger,

00:06:15.078 --> 00:06:18.140
and then I became an actor, which was like

00:06:18.140 --> 00:06:21.057
a coat hanger that talks, and then,

00:06:22.462 --> 00:06:25.086
and then I ultimately just came full loop,

00:06:25.086 --> 00:06:28.106
it came back around, and
by the way, when you talk,

00:06:28.106 --> 00:06:30.015
you're generally not even
speaking your own words,

00:06:30.015 --> 00:06:32.008
you're speaking somebody else's words,

00:06:32.008 --> 00:06:34.485
and try to make it sound really real.

00:06:34.485 --> 00:06:35.669
And I then did full loop around

00:06:35.669 --> 00:06:39.206
and started looking at technology again,

00:06:39.206 --> 00:06:40.844
and started getting
interested in technology,

00:06:40.844 --> 00:06:43.080
and started getting
interested in engineering,

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:47.437
and started really getting
interested in solving problems.

00:06:47.437 --> 00:06:50.038
Solving my own problems, and then solving

00:06:50.038 --> 00:06:52.176
other people's problems and
what the value of that is,

00:06:52.176 --> 00:06:54.823
and so whether it's
technology or philanthropy,

00:06:54.823 --> 00:06:59.529
or actually even performing,
or being an actor,

00:06:59.529 --> 00:07:02.378
I started to make it
about solving problems.

00:07:02.378 --> 00:07:04.862
And I think that that's probably

00:07:04.862 --> 00:07:07.801
the single biggest thing
that, as an angel investor

00:07:07.801 --> 00:07:12.482
I look for in entrepreneurs,
is people who genuinely

00:07:12.482 --> 00:07:14.963
want to solve a problem, a real problem,

00:07:14.963 --> 00:07:17.252
a problem that exists
not just for themselves,

00:07:17.252 --> 00:07:18.817
but sometimes just for themselves

00:07:18.817 --> 00:07:22.265
and then it sort of turns
into this wave effect

00:07:22.265 --> 00:07:24.511
that solves other people's problems.

00:07:24.511 --> 00:07:27.045
And sometimes by trying
to solve your own problem,

00:07:27.045 --> 00:07:29.969
you're solving everybody else's problems.

00:07:29.969 --> 00:07:32.772
'Cause generally, if you
want to affect the world,

00:07:32.772 --> 00:07:34.538
you have to change yourself first.

00:07:34.538 --> 00:07:36.425
Everybody tries to go
out and change the world

00:07:36.425 --> 00:07:38.689
by changing other people and other things,

00:07:38.689 --> 00:07:41.399
but the real way to do it
is by changing yourself,

00:07:41.399 --> 00:07:44.743
and sometimes making those
really uncomfortable choices

00:07:44.743 --> 00:07:46.507
to go up against everyone in your life

00:07:46.507 --> 00:07:50.674
that you look up to, and take
that risk to go be a model,

00:07:53.919 --> 00:07:56.769
or go do this thing that
nobody else is doing.

00:07:56.769 --> 00:07:59.601
- Basically, what success meant to me

00:07:59.601 --> 00:08:02.351
was the opportunity to be useful,

00:08:04.556 --> 00:08:08.556
so somebody else might
entertain me as a choice,

00:08:13.330 --> 00:08:17.080
but it also meant that
I could use that event

00:08:22.380 --> 00:08:25.250
of the success to do something different

00:08:25.250 --> 00:08:27.070
than what was expected.

00:08:27.070 --> 00:08:29.358
I could go to another genre, I could go

00:08:29.358 --> 00:08:31.979
to another kind of role.

00:08:31.979 --> 00:08:35.670
I could go from the success of Witness

00:08:35.670 --> 00:08:39.396
to the opportunity of Mosquito Coast.

00:08:39.396 --> 00:08:42.396
And people say, "Woo, wait a minute.

00:08:46.446 --> 00:08:48.846
"Might not be the best career
choice," and I thought,

00:08:48.846 --> 00:08:53.011
well, I don't have to make
the best career choice,

00:08:53.011 --> 00:08:57.229
I have to make a choice
that will challenge me

00:08:57.229 --> 00:08:59.396
and be interesting for me.

